        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What accessories do I need for a new treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Start with a mat and the lubricant your manual specifies, because they protect the floor and the machine. Add a fan for longer sessions, a chest strap if you train to heart-rate zones, and a foot pod if you train to pace. Buy a surge protector only if your manual permits or recommends one.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should I plug my treadmill into a surge protector?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if your manual allows it. Some manufacturers recommend a UL 1449-listed surge suppressor rated for 15 amps; others say to plug directly into a grounded wall outlet. Never use an ordinary power strip or an extension cord, plug nothing else into the same device, and ask a licensed electrician about any wiring or circuit question.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What lubricant should I use on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most home treadmills, 100% silicone, applied every 40 to 50 hours of use. Check the manual first: some decks are pre-waxed or maintenance-free and must not receive silicone, and some manufacturers specify their own product. Never use petroleum-based sprays, household oils or belt dressings under a treadmill belt.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a chest strap better than a treadmill grip sensor?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Grip sensors only read while you hold the rails, readings jump as your grip changes, and holding on alters how you walk or run. A chest strap reads continuously and is the reference standard for home training. Check whether your treadmill or app accepts Bluetooth or ANT+ straps before buying one.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do I need a foot pod for Zwift on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if your treadmill cannot send its speed to Zwift. Some connected treadmills broadcast speed directly, and then a foot pod adds little. For any other machine, a foot pod clipped to your shoe is the universal way to get your pace into Zwift. A phone or watch can work too, but is generally less precise.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best way to clean a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Unplug it, then wipe the rails, console and belt with a barely damp microfibre cloth, using a little mild soap only where needed, and dry everything afterwards. Vacuum around and under the machine regularly. Avoid bleach, ammonia, solvents, oily wipes and spraying anything directly onto the console or belt.
            </p>
